  "text": "To solve this problem, let's reason step by step.\n\n1. **Identify Midpoints and Areas:**\n   - \\( E \\) is the midpoint of \\( \\overline{AB} \\), so \\( AE = EB \\).\n   - \\( F \\) is the midpoint of \\( \\overline{BC} \\), so \\( BF = FC \\).\n   - \\( G \\) is the midpoint of \\( \\overline{CD} \\), so \\( CG = GD \\).\n\n2. **Area of Triangles:**\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) is given as 3 square units.\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle EFG \\) is given as 2 square units.\n\n3. **Area of Quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\):**\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) and \\( \\triangle EFG \\) together form a part of the quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\).\n\n4. **Area of \\( \\triangle CEG \\):**\n   - Since \\( E \\), \\( F \\), and \\( G \\) are midpoints, the areas of the triangles formed by these midpoints are proportional to the areas of the triangles formed by the vertices of the quadrilateral.\n\n5. **Area Calculation:**\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) and \\( \\triangle EFG \\) together is \\( 3 + 2 = 5 \\) square units.\n   - Since \\( E \\), \\( F \\), and \\( G \\) are midpoints, the area of \\( \\triangle CEG \\) is the remaining part of the quadrilateral after removing \\( \\triangle AEF \\) and \\( \\triangle EFG \\).\n\n6. **Final Area:**\n   - The area of \\( \\triangle CEG \\) is the area of the quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\) minus the areas of \\( \\triangle AEF \\) and \\( \\triangle EFG \\).\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( \\triangle CEG \\) is \\( 13 \\) square units.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).",
